so this week our cover story looks at  the conflict that's flared up between  India and Pakistan and in particular how  this puts India's Prime Minister  Narendra Modi on the spot this is just  the latest of many many terror attacks  that have taken place inside India  conducted by groups based in Pakistan  two weeks ago there was a terrible  terrorist attack in the Indian  controlled part of Kashmir 40 Indian  soldiers were killed in recent days mr.  Modi has ordered Indian airplanes into  Pakistani airspace to bomb targets there  in retaliation an aerial battle ensued  Pakistan and India both claimed to have  shot down one another's planes Pakistan  captured an Indian pilot this is the  first time since 1971 that planes have  been crossing into the airspace of the  the other country and bombing targets  within it but of course this time around  India and Pakistan both have nuclear  weapons so the situation is that much  more dangerous the big question now is  whether the two sides are able to back  down or whether the sort of spiral of  escalation continues the thing that  makes this situation particularly  combustible is the fact that India is  facing an election in April at the last  election campaign mr. Modi  sold himself as a big reformer who would  bring jobs to India that hasn't quite  worked out as planned though the economy  has grown unemployment has risen and as  a sort of broad dissatisfaction amongst  a lot of voters with the state of the  economy and then he also has as his sort  of foot soldiers in the campaign Hindu  nationalist activists who feel that mr.  Modi hasn't done enough to advance a  strong Pro Hindu anti-muslim agenda  Hindus are 80% of the population in  India and obviously the temptation for  any politician is to try to appeal to  them because that will automatically  help at the ballot box  mr. Modi is an avowed li nationalist  politician he has a particularly  carefully cultivated such tough-guy  persona there's all kinds of pressure on  him to to try and appear resolute and  strong not back down teach the  Pakistanis a lesson that's the kind of  rhetoric he uses all the time on the  campaign trail and that pressure  obviously could could result in a  stronger reaction that could lead the  two countries further down this this  spiral of conflict having said that  there are some signs that neither  country wants the conflict to escalate  Western countries have tried to sort of  take India on to a different course of  diplomatic course try to raise this in  the Security Council get Kandam nations  of the terrorist group and of Pakistan  support for it there in the Security  Council to try and bring this on to a  diplomatic track and and and defuse the  tensions and certainly you know for the  safety of India that's the thing that  mr. Modi needs to do he needs to step  back from the conflict
 

